 the the beginner period  where you're you feel like you're making  quite a lot of progress and you're  struggling but you're seeing the results  of your effort and then there's that  long long period where you are gradually  increasing your familiarity  words that you you know learn and forget  and learn and forget that eventually  start to stick and so that that sort of  comfortable competence competence in the  language takes a long time  so i guess the gist of it is  what you do at the beginning whether you  speak don't speak  whether you i mean certainly watch  movies to stimulate you but you won't be  learning the language from it  uh  you know but and do have a look at the  sort of advice that people have put in  different blogs but but all of this what  it does  it gets you to commit to the language  and depending on your personality you  may be  you know better you know able to commit  to the language if you can start  speaking early  or if you are into music  or anything like that that gets you  motivated to get out the language but  you will find that it it almost doesn't  matter what you do at the beginning in  my view and i don't believe that you can  ingrain mistakes or any of this stuff  i genuinely believe because i've started  languages in different ways  and it is such a long haul  that when i think back you know the  amount of time spent in that initial  beginner stage is relatively small now i  recognize that a majority of people  never ever leave the beginner stage they  never get beyond that level where they  can say a few things and don't really  understand too well what people say back  at them never achieve that level of  comfort  so i would say to people recognize that  the important thing  is not what you do at the beginning the  important thing is that you stay with it  and you stay with it for the long haul  because it does take a lot of time to  achieve that level of of proficiency and  comprehension that i think is is the  mark of flue